  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM EST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nLow pressure complex deepening to ~996mb will continue to make \ninroads north and west through the Ohio Valley before turning \noutward to the east with time through the day today. Anticipate\nnegligible impacts across a large majority of the CWA as a \nsubstantial amount of low level dry air hampers moisture intrusion.\nFor the time being, surface high pressure essentially curving \noutside the periphery of the low pressure complex continues its \nrather suppressing properties of precipitation, with a stark cutoff\nessentially setting up a gradient across the Saginaw Bay region. The\nvast majority of ongoing trends and model guidance continues to show\nexquisite rainfall totals confined to the Lake Huron / Saginaw Bay\nregion, where shore locales may see up to 1.00\" of rain thru today.\nMeanwhile, dry air will hamper precip development the farther west\nyou go, leading to drier weather in NW lower and eastern upper.\nSnowfall set to mix in across areas away from the shores this AM;\nquestions regarding accumulation are minute at this point, as the\nupper end amounts guidance showcases are generally <1\", primarily\naffecting a corridor stretching from roughly Mio to Gladwin. Precip\ndoes eventually taper by afternoon in these areas.\n\nBeyond this system, the rest of the weather pattern turns mundane, \noutside of some quick wave passages within a zonal flow regime \novertaking the upper pattern. Moisture starved systems are the rule. \nDuring Saturday morning, we start off dry before a weak perturbation \narrives late morning into the afternoon with light rain showers. \nBarring some ludicrous upward trend in moisture, certainly not \nexactly trending toward a washout, with ample dry time anticipated \nacross the region. The next system set to impact northern Michigan \nreaches the area Sunday, with better moisture concentration favoring \nsouthern portions of the area, where best rain chances thus reside. \n\nGoing into next week, cold air behind the Sunday system brings the \nonset of lake effect snow with NW flow and crashing 850mb temps. \nLake induced snows probably trend downward quickly Tuesday as an \nincoming wave arriving Wednesday drums up southerly return flow. The \noverwhelming consensus from guidance with this system shows another \nnuisance period of light rain and snow showers anticipated. A larger \nsystem may pass through late next week, with mixed precip potential.",
